From e957cb38b75d8a99714ed0522ae959f0f89621ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(camera): render core-resolved server camera movement

Subscribe to ViaBedrock's versioned camera fade channel on direct and
ViaProxy connections. Draw the supplied color and opacity timeline while
preserving the HUD, including when the normal HUD is hidden. Render the
remaining elapsed state after late registration instead of restarting it.

Fade merging and protocol decoding belong to core. The add-on only owns
the client clock and overlay extraction, and releases state at disconnect.
Aim assistance remains a separate requirement. Native screenshot comparison
must establish overlay order.

Apply server FOV to the world camera before projection and culling setup.
Use the shared core transition rules with the previous rendered projection
and the unmodified local projection, preserving native overshoot and clear
lifecycle. Queue commands until a projection context exists and restore
elapsed snapshots without replaying the effect from its beginning. Keep
the separate first-person hand projection and release state at disconnect.

Subscribe to the shared camera shake state. Apply world-space position and
pitch/yaw offsets after ordinary camera alignment and before culling.
Keep entity aim and outbound movement unchanged. Add the native Allow
Camera Shake preference, ignore new shakes while disabled, consume stop,
and release all state on disconnect. Core owns queues and noise sampling.

Apply core-resolved free-camera targets with the shared native blend
updater. Supply the previous rendered frame on interrupted transitions,
apply the pose before shake and culling, and select server perspectives
without changing the saved user view. Clear and disconnect release the
override. Ordinary player movement and outbound aim remain unchanged.

Restore settled targets for late subscriptions instead of synthesizing a
new transition from an unknown old frame. Built-in first/third-person
alignment currently uses Java's camera collision and distance rules, so
activation works but those poses do not establish Bedrock camera parity.
Native parity for audio/effects, follow cameras and attached targets remains incomplete.

Subscribe to the independent tracking snapshot and update every free-camera
instance before the active blend. Resolve target unique IDs and lifetimes
from native actor state, then interpolate the native physical box center
and actor yaw for each rendered frame. Clear tracking on disconnect.
Position commands retain their own sequence, so a target update does not
restart the position ease. Physical bounds come from the shared actor
bridge rather than Java proxy dimensions.

Negotiate the shared follow-camera channel and resolve local players and
explicit attachment actors from native identities and physical bounds.
Advance follow instances before the active blend, preserve pending poses
while actor state loads, and render the resulting detached camera before
shake and culling. Clear its state on disconnect.

Forward accepted Java mouse-turn deltas to the active follow instance.
Existing camera-input permission handling still controls the collector.
Core comparisons establish composed component math; native avoidance,
activation/pivot rules, input/body coupling and visual parity remain open.

Bind follow-camera avoidance to the translated client world using the
shared native tracer. Supply custom collision eligibility and selection
hit bounds from converted metadata, preserve extra-layer liquids, and
use existing block geometry for ordinary states. Sample solid-origin
collapse before casting the eight corner rays.

Native component and world comparisons establish tracer and follow-frame
arithmetic. Ordinary block geometry and solid-state fallbacks still need
per-type native validation; these bindings do not establish complete
visible camera parity.

Negotiate named and inline spline programs and supply them to the shared free-camera
instance runtime. Render their positions and quaternion rotations through
the existing blend, before shake, culling and listener extraction. Release
program state at disconnect. Core owns curve construction, keyframe easing
and frame evaluation. The shared tracking codec negotiates its extended
position revisions on a separate version-two channel. Native system
ordering and official-client visible parity still require verification.

Negotiate version two of the spline channel. Live commands retain each
running instance's keyframes while replacing its curve and duration;
snapshots install the complete retained program without appending again.
Empty replacement channels reuse running frames. Expiry releases instance
programs, and accepted transport payloads do not retain retired history.
Native component probes establish replacement and retirement behavior;
complete frame scheduling and official-client visible parity remain open.

Receive negotiated aim catalogs, command settings, actor priorities,
control bytes and selected camera context through bounded fragments.
Install only fully decoded views. Scope queued work to the captured
connection and disconnect generation, and resolve actor metadata bindings
only against matching native lifetimes. Clear accepted and pending state
on disconnect without letting late removals affect replacement actors.

An actual add-on connection verifies six setting transitions, five control
bytes, camera context and clear, target removal/replacement and normal
cleanup. Native world queries, selection, reticles and
interaction rotation remain incomplete; transport does not establish them.

Report the current complete aim view sequence and perspective to core.
Use the local camera option without a server preset; forced first-person
is ineligible, while rendered third-person, free and follow cameras permit
aim activation. Reset client request state on disconnect. Core validates
snapshot and camera identity and sends native packet 321, leaving settings
resolution to BDS rather than synthesizing optional camera aim defaults.

Two actual client recordings verify exact request/reply sequences,
complete/empty/partial aim objects, native command restoration and normal
cleanup. Reticle, target/world queries, interaction rotation, local F5
transitions and full native event scheduling remain open.

Consume the generalized attached-camera rendering mode and negotiated
camera_follow_v2 state. Fixed-boom and orbit presets use the shared core
frame runtime before blend, shake, projection, culling and sound-listener
extraction. Fixed presets retain orientation across actor rotation and
ignore orbit input. The existing native actor identity, lifetime, bounds
and origin-offset producers remain shared. Their complete native pivot
and world geometry semantics still need comparison; this integration does
not establish full native camera or pixel parity.

Keep native attachment identity separate from render-sample readiness.
An actor with a native lifetime but pending Java entity or matching native
bounds remains a known actor with no ready frame sample. Only disappearance
of the native identity is reported as actor loss. This lets the shared
runtime retire failed fixed-boom attachments without treating delayed
render metadata as removal, while preserving existing target-tracker
behavior until its physical center is available.
